    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2956973b-86f0-54c8-b6f6-3e8ed5f67bca",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\n- **I.** $y' - \\sqrt{x} \\cdot y = \\frac{x^{3}}{y}$\n- **II.** $y' + y \\cdot \\tan{\\left(x \\right)} = - \\sqrt{x} \\cdot y^{\\frac{3}{2}}$\n- **III.** $y' - y^{2} \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)} = y^{6}$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation I is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=- \\sqrt{x},\\quad q(x)=x^{3},\\quad n=-1.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\tan{\\left(x \\right)},\\quad q(x)=- \\sqrt{x},\\quad n=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\nTherefore, select I, II.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
