      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\n- **I.** $y' - y^{2} \\cdot e^{x} = \\sqrt{y}$\n- **II.** $y^{3}y' + y^{4} \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)} = \\sqrt{y}$\n- **III.** $yy' + x \\cdot \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} = \\cos^{2}{\\left(y \\right)}$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $y^{3}$, we get\n\n$$\ny' + y \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)} = \\frac{1}{y^{\\frac{5}{2}}}\n$$\n\n In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\log{\\left(x \\right)},\\quad q(x)=1,\\quad n=- \\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $y$, we get\n\n$$\ny' + \\frac{x \\cdot \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}}{y} = \\frac{\\cos^{2}{\\left(y \\right)}}{y}\n$$\n\n The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\nTherefore, select II.",
