      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\n- **I.** $\\sqrt[3]{y}y' + \\sqrt{x} \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{y} = y^{3}$\n- **II.** $y' - y \\cdot \\sec{\\left(x \\right)} = \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$\n- **III.** $yy' + \\sqrt{x} \\cdot y^{2} = 1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $\\sqrt[3]{y}$, we get\n\n$$\ny' + \\sqrt{x} = y^{\\frac{8}{3}}\n$$\n\n The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\n- Equation III is a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $y$, we get\n\n$$\ny' + \\sqrt{x} \\cdot y = \\frac{1}{y}\n$$\n\n In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\quad q(x)=1,\\quad n=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, select III.",
